    "body": "<div class=\"nigfacts\" style=\"--fb:#F8D898;--fbr:#FAB325;--ftx:#5B471E\"><div class=\"nigfact\" style=\"--fc:#5B471E\"><img class=\"fe fe-img\" alt=\"\" src=\"/resources/assets/img/ddff958da034dbf9.png\"><span class=\"ft\">Composed in תשמ״ג‎ (1982) by Reb Feitel Levin &amp; Reb Avrohom Charitonov.</span></div><div class=\"nigfact\" style=\"--fc:#5B471E\"><img class=\"fe fe-img\" alt=\"\" src=\"/resources/assets/img/a5b672aa1dfbe68b.png\"><span class=\"ft\">First sung by Chassidim in 770 by the Rosh Chodesh Kislev farbrengen in תשמ״ג‎ (1982).</span></div><div class=\"nigfact\" style=\"--fc:#5B471E\"><img class=\"fe fe-img\" alt=\"\" src=\"/resources/assets/img/75570d1082f1c751.png\"><span class=\"ft\">This lebedike march celebrates the Rebbe returning home from 770 for the first time after his heart attack in תשל״ח‎ (1977).</span></div></div><h3>The Happiest-Feeling Niggun</h3><p>Tzvi is playing his piccolo with intensity—tapping his feet, nodding his head, humming to himself—but no one can hear. That’s because he’s playing alone in the attic. He’s working on the Rosh Chodesh Kislev niggun, a melody so pumped with joy that if his siblings heard it, they would come running in a second.</p><p>attack on Shemini Atzeres.</p><p>Tzvi begins to smile and to tap his feet faster. I’m getting it right! I wonder if being b’simcha is helping.</p><p>But Tzvi wants to be alone so he can focus on getting the niggun just right. It’s taking so long that he’s starting to get frustrated.</p><p>His heart suddenly feels light. Simcha is always there between a Rebbe and his Chassidim.</p><p>He tries to relax his mind. He imagines how joyous the Chassidim must have felt on Rosh Chodesh Kislev, when the Rebbe returned to</p><p>770 after his heart</p><p>He should probably share his joy with others...</p>",
    "source": "Source: Parshas Chayei Sara · 26 Cheshvan 5787"
